
Assignment: Build an API-Driven Search UI

Create a new page on your GitHub Pages site that lets a user search for real data from a public API.
Assignment Goal

Build a small web application that allows a user to type a search term, click a button, and see results pulled from a public API.

Your project should combine the skills from this unit:

    fetch()
    Promises and async/await
    JSON data
    query parameters
    loading states
    error handling
    retry-friendly user experience

What You Are Building

You will create a new page on your existing GitHub Pages site for this assignment. That page should include:

    a page title and short instructions
    a search input
    a search button
    a results area
    a loading message while the request is running
    an error message if something fails
    a clean layout using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript

Requirements

    Use your existing GitHub Pages site
    Create a new HTML page for this assignment
    Use a public API
    Allow the user to enter a search term
    Use that search term in the API request
    Display at least 3 pieces of information from the returned data
    Use fetch() with async/await
    Show a loading state
    Show an error state
    Handle blank input
    Deploy the finished page to GitHub Pages

Suggested API Options

You may choose another instructor-approved public API, but these are good beginner-friendly options:
```
Option 1: JSONPlaceholder

Best for: searching posts, users, comments, or todos for practice

Link: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/ Links to an external site.

Why it is good: simple JSON, easy endpoints, great for learning fetch and search/filter logic

Example idea: let users search posts by user ID or browse users and display name, email, and company
Option 2: Open-Meteo

Best for: weather search projects

Link: https://open-meteo.com/en/docs Links to an external site.

Why it is good: no API key required for common educational use, real data, strong documentation

Example idea: let users search for a city, then show temperature, wind speed, and forecast data

Note: this option is slightly more advanced because city-name search often works best with a geocoding step first.
Option 3: TheMealDB

Best for: recipe or meal search projects

Link: https://www.themealdb.com/api.php Links to an external site.

Why it is good: easy search endpoints, visual data, great for beginner UI projects

Example idea: let users search for a meal name and display the meal title, category, area, and image
```
Recommended Project Ideas

    Recipe Search: search by meal name and display recipe information
    Weather Lookup: search by city and display current weather details
    User Finder: search users or posts and display selected details

Easiest option for most students: TheMealDB or JSONPlaceholder.
